@.str.7 = private unnamed_addr constant [2734 x i8] c"`/-- ... -/ #guard_msgs in cmd` captures the messages generated by the command `cmd`\0Aand checks that they match the contents of the docstring.\0A\0ABasic example:\0A```lean\0A/--\0Aerror: unknown identifier 'x'\0A-/\0A#guard_msgs in\0Aexample : \CE\B1 := x\0A```\0AThis checks that there is such an error and then consumes the message.\0A\0ABy default, the command captures all messages, but the filter condition can be adjusted.\0AFor example, we can select only warnings:\0A```lean\0A/--\0Awarning: declaration uses 'sorry'\0A-/\0A#guard_msgs(warning) in\0Aexample : \CE\B1 := sorry\0A```\0Aor only errors\0A```lean\0A#guard_msgs(error) in\0Aexample : \CE\B1 := sorry\0A```\0AIn the previous example, since warnings are not captured there is a warning on `sorry`.\0AWe can drop the warning completely with\0A```lean\0A#guard_msgs(error, drop warning) in\0Aexample : \CE\B1 := sorry\0A```\0A\0AIn general, `#guard_msgs` accepts a comma-separated list of configuration clauses in parentheses:\0A```\0A#guard_msgs (configElt,*) in cmd\0A```\0ABy default, the configuration list is `(check all, whitespace := normalized, ordering := exact)`.\0A\0AMessage filters select messages by severity:\0A- `info`, `warning`, `error`: (non-trace) messages with the given severity level.\0A- `trace`: trace messages\0A- `all`: all messages.\0A\0AThe filters can be prefixed with the action to take:\0A- `check` (the default): capture and check the message\0A- `drop`: drop the message\0A- `pass`: let the message pass through\0A\0AIf no filter is specified, `check all` is assumed.  Otherwise, these filters are processed in\0Aleft-to-right order, with an implicit `pass all` at the end.\0A\0AWhitespace handling (after trimming leading and trailing whitespace):\0A- `whitespace := exact` requires an exact whitespace match.\0A- `whitespace := normalized` converts all newline characters to a space before matching\0A  (the default). This allows breaking long lines.\0A- `whitespace := lax` collapses whitespace to a single space before matching.\0A\0AMessage ordering:\0A- `ordering := exact` uses the exact ordering of the messages (the default).\0A- `ordering := sorted` sorts the messages in lexicographic order.\0A  This helps with testing commands that are non-deterministic in their ordering.\0A\0AFor example, `#guard_msgs (error, drop all) in cmd` means to check warnings and drop\0Aeverything else.\0A\0AThe command elaborator has special support for `#guard_msgs` for linting.\0AThe `#guard_msgs` itself wants to capture linter warnings,\0Aso it elaborates the command it is attached to as if it were a top-level command.\0AHowever, the command elaborator runs linters for *all* top-level commands,\0Awhich would include `#guard_msgs` itself, and would cause duplicate and/or uncaptured linter warnings.\0AThe top-level command elaborator only runs the linters if `#guard_msgs` is not present.\0A\00", align 1

